<SummerLayout
  title="Japan and the Zainichi — Pachinko"
  section="pachinko"
  spoke="history"
>
  <div class="sr-spoke-header">
    <span class="sr-spoke-header-eyebrow">Pachinko  ·  Spoke 03 of 05</span>
    <h1 class="sr-spoke-header-title">Japan and the Zainichi</h1>
    <p class="sr-spoke-header-deck">How colonization made an invisible community — from annexation through the postwar legal strip.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="sr-spoke-content">

    <p class="sr-lede"><em>Pachinko</em> eschews explanation of the history that shaped its characters, focusing instead on what it felt like to live inside history. The historical forces behind the novel are worth understanding on their own terms as the machinery that produced the specific conditions that shape the lives of every character in the novel.</p>

    <h2>How Japan Took Korea</h2>

    <p>Korea did not fall quickly. Japan dismantled it methodically over decades.</p>

    <p>Japan forced Korea to open its ports through the unequal Treaty of Ganghwa in 1876 — the first in a series of agreements that progressively stripped Korea of sovereignty while maintaining the appearance of an independent state. By 1905, following Japan's victory in the Russo-Japanese War, Korea was compelled to become a protectorate. The protectorate period was colonial rule in everything but name: Japan controlled Korea's foreign policy, its military, and its civil administration, while Korean officials retained ceremonial positions with no real authority.</p>

    <p>Formal annexation came on August 29, 1910. The treaty was signed not by Emperor Sunjong himself — he had no meaningful choice in the matter — but by Prime Minister Yi Wan-yong, who served as Japan's collaborator and became the most reviled figure in modern Korean history. Japan's Resident-General had suppressed all Korean newspapers in the days before signing to prevent public knowledge of what was being negotiated. Korea was renamed <em>Chōsen</em> — the Japanese reading of Joseon — and governed by a Governor-General, always a Japanese military officer appointed directly by the Japanese Emperor.</p>

    <p>The annexation's legal validity has been disputed ever since. International law at the time recognized that treaties signed under duress lacked validity, and the Korean emperor had no independent authority. When Japan and South Korea normalized relations in 1965, the compromise language declared all pre-1910 treaties "already null and void" — language that was deliberately ambiguous about whether they were invalid from the start or merely rescinded. Japan has resisted the former interpretation; Korea has insisted on it. This disagreement over language is fundamentally a dispute over whether Japan owes an apology, reparations, and a full accounting of what it did.</p>

    <h2>What Japan Extracted from Korea</h2>

    <p>The colonial economy was reorganized entirely to serve Japanese interests; its most consequential instrument was the <strong>Land Survey of 1910–1918</strong>.</p>

    <p>The survey required all landowners to formally register their land under a new Japanese property rights system. The stated rationale was modernization, but the effect was mass dispossession. Korean farmers whose families had cultivated land for generations under customary tenure arrangements that did not map onto the new legal system lost that land when they failed to register correctly. Village commons and clan-owned land which belonged to no single individual and could not be registered were expropriated by the colonial government and transferred cheaply to Japanese buyers. By the end of the colonial period, Japanese individuals and corporations owned roughly one quarter of Korea's agricultural land.</p>

    <p>The colonial government also reorganized Korean agriculture around Japanese consumption. Rice production was dramatically expanded — Korean farmers were ordered to convert dry fields to paddy fields — and the rice was exported to Japan. Koreans were left to subsist on lower-quality cereals imported from Manchuria in place of their own harvest. By the 1930s, approximately 70% of Korean agricultural workers had been reduced to tenant status, paying rents of 50–70% of their harvest to Japanese and Japanese-allied Korean landlords.</p>

    <p>The combination of land dispossession, rack-renting, and agricultural crisis drove the migration that created the Zainichi community. Koreans fled to Japan because economic systems at home had been systematically destroyed or redirected to serve Japanese needs. The approximately two million Koreans who emigrated to Japan during the colonial period were not voluntary migrants in any meaningful sense.</p>

    <h2>The March First Movement, 1919</h2>

    <p>By 1919, nearly a decade of military suppression had pushed Korean resentment to a breaking point. Two catalysts converged: the death of former Emperor Gojong, which brought mourners to Seoul from across the country, and Woodrow Wilson's Fourteen Points speech, which had introduced the principle of national self-determination to a world audience. Korean organizers had already begun preparing petitions to the Paris Peace Conference.</p>

    <p>On March 1, 1919, thirty-three cultural and religious leaders — Protestant ministers, Buddhists, and members of the indigenous Cheondoist faith — gathered in Seoul and read aloud a Korean Declaration of Independence, modeled partly on the American Declaration. Copies had been distributed throughout the country the night before. The demonstrations spread instantly and nationwide: an estimated two million Koreans participated in more than 1,500 separate demonstrations over the following weeks and months.</p>

    <p>Japan's response was violent and oppressive. Approximately 7,500 Koreans were killed; an estimated 46,000 were arrested. Japanese forces burned buildings, including churches and schools. Yu Gwan-sun, a teenage student who organized demonstrations in her home province after being expelled from school, was arrested, tortured, and died in prison at seventeen. She remains one of the most celebrated figures of the Korean independence movement.</p>

    <p>The March First Movement failed in its immediate goal: Japan did not grant independence. But it forced Japan to shift from overt military rule to a slightly more flexible "cultural rule" in the 1920s, and it hardened Korean national consciousness in ways that persisted through the colonial period and beyond. In <em>Pachinko</em>, Isak's older brother Sameol dies in connection with these events; his death is a reminder, present in the novel's background, of the cost of resistance.</p>

    <h2>Wartime Conscription and the Migration to Japan</h2>

    <p>The migration of Koreans to Japan that created the Zainichi community operated through three overlapping mechanisms, growing more coercive as the war years advanced:</p>

    <p><strong>Boshū (recruitment):</strong> Japanese companies recruited Korean workers, sometimes through misrepresentation — promising good wages and conditions that did not exist.</p>

    <p><strong>Kan-assen (government coordination):</strong> The government directed recruitment to fill specific labor quotas, sometimes by force.</p>

    <p><strong>Chōyō (conscription):</strong> Formal conscription under the National Mobilization Law, extended to Korea in 1942, with criminal penalties for refusal.</p>

