    <p>By the end of World War II, approximately 5.4 million Koreans had been mobilized for Japan's war effort. Roughly 670,000 were relocated to mainland Japan for factory and mine labor under conditions most historians describe as appalling. An estimated 60,000 died between 1939 and 1945.</p>

    <p>When Japan surrendered in August 1945, approximately two million Koreans were living in Japan. Most eventually repatriated, but about 600,000 remained, unable to return to a peninsula immediately divided between American and Soviet occupation zones or unwilling to abandon the businesses and community networks they had built over years. These 600,000 and their descendants are the Zainichi community.</p>

    <h2>The Postwar Legal Strip: From Subjects to Stateless</h2>

    <p>Japan's treatment of the Zainichi community after 1945 is one of the most precisely documented cases of systematic legal dispossession in modern history.</p>

    <p>In December 1945, Korean voting rights were revoked on the grounds that Koreans were not Japanese. In 1947, the Alien Registration Ordinance classified Koreans as foreigners, despite the fact that they were still technically Japanese nationals. The Japanese government maintained both positions simultaneously: Koreans were too foreign to vote, but too Japanese to establish their own ethnic schools.</p>

    <p>The decisive rupture came in 1952, when the San Francisco Peace Treaty took effect and formally ended the Allied occupation of Japan. As a direct consequence, Zainichi Koreans were unilaterally stripped of Japanese nationality without being given the opportunity to choose between Japanese and Korean citizenship, as international law would normally require. The mechanism was a single administrative notice, issued without legislation or consultation. Overnight, 600,000 people became legally stateless.</p>

    <p>The practical consequences were immediate and comprehensive:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Employment:</strong> All public-sector positions — government offices, the national railway, the postal service — were closed to non-citizens. Private-sector employers who had been willing to hire Koreans often required them to use Japanese names and conceal their origin.</li>
      <li><strong>Welfare:</strong> Medical insurance, pension, child allowance, and public housing were all restricted to citizens. Zainichi Koreans paid taxes on the same basis as Japanese citizens; they received none of the social infrastructure those taxes funded.</li>
      <li><strong>Employment in practice:</strong> In 1952, 61% of Zainichi Koreans were unemployed. The largest recorded occupation (6.6% of the community) was day laboring (<em>nikoyon</em>). Others worked as rag-pickers (<em>bataya</em>), black market traders, food stall vendors, and home brewers of <em>doburoku</em> — occupations Japanese workers avoided, which is precisely why they were available.</li>
      <li><strong>Registration:</strong> Zainichi Koreans were required to carry alien registration cards at all times and produce them on police demand. From 1955, all registered foreigners were required to be fingerprinted.</li>
      <li><strong>Education:</strong> After liberation in 1945, the Zainichi community rebuilt Korean-language schooling from nothing. Within three years, 600 ethnic schools were operating across Japan, enrolling approximately 60,000 children.
        <p>In January 1948, the Japanese government ordered these schools closed. On April 24, 1948, approximately 15,000 people — students, teachers, and parents — assembled near the Hyogo prefectural office in Kobe. Hundreds entered the building and forced the governor to revoke the closure order. General Douglas MacArthur's GHQ declared a state of emergency; the Eighth Army commander called the protest rioting. Around 1,700 people were arrested; 136 faced military trial. Students sent postcards addressed directly to MacArthur and his deputy Eichelberger demanding the release of their teachers. Those postcards survive in the U.S. National Archives.</p>
        <p>Zainichi children who attended Japanese public schools in the years that followed faced widespread discrimination from teachers and classmates.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>The legal status of the community remained in flux for decades. The 1965 normalization treaty between Japan and South Korea gave South Korea-affiliated Zainichi special permanent residency — security without citizenship. Those affiliated with North Korea, or affiliated with neither state, remained in legal limbo until 1982, when Japan ratified international human rights covenants that required it to extend permanent residency more broadly. The fingerprinting requirement was partially abolished in 1991–1992 — but only for Special Permanent Residents, the South Korea-affiliated Zainichi. Full abolition for all long-term foreign residents did not come until April 2000.</p>

