    <p>Each milestone in this table is a legal reform the Zainichi community fought for, often over decades. None of them resolved the fundamental condition: Zainichi Koreans remained, at every point in this timeline, a community whose legal standing in the only country they had ever lived in was conditional, incomplete, and subject to revision.</p>

    <h2>The Pachinko Industry: Exclusion as Foundation</h2>

    <p>The pachinko industry's relationship to the Zainichi community is a direct consequence of the discrimination described above — and a precise illustration of how systematic exclusion shapes economic life.</p>

    <p>The same structural logic operated across every economic margin available to the community. Rag-picking (<em>bataya</em>) — collecting discarded rags, newspapers, and scrap metal — was entered by Zainichi Koreans for the identical reason as pachinko: Japanese workers avoided it, and exclusion from formal employment left it as one of the few options open.</p>

    <p>Pachinko originated as a children's street game in the 1920s and reappeared in 1946 as commercial parlors opened in postwar Japan's economic chaos. Zainichi Koreans began entering the industry around 1947 — not because they were drawn to it, but because it was one of the few businesses in which they could find employment. Barred from public-sector work and facing discrimination in most formal private-sector employment, they operated in the economic margins the system left open.</p>

    <p>Pachinko occupied precisely such a margin. The industry exists in a legal gray area: gambling is formally prohibited in Japan, but pachinko circumvents this through a prize-exchange mechanism; players win tokens or prizes that are then exchanged for cash at separate kiosks nearby. Japanese owners were reluctant to invest heavily in a business that operated in this quasi-legal space. When a 1954 regulatory change caused a significant industry contraction (the number of parlors collapsed from 50,000 to 9,000), Japanese owners could and did leave for other work. With fewer alternatives, Zainichi Koreans stayed. As the industry recovered and grew, Korean ownership expanded dramatically.</p>

    <p>By the 1990s, pachinko had become a 19 trillion yen annual industry — roughly twice the export revenues of Japan's entire car industry — and Zainichi Koreans accounted for approximately 90% of sales. The industry funded community organizations, ethnic schools, and political advocacy groups affiliated with both South and North Korea. As Min Jin Lee has written, it was "one of the very few businesses in which Koreans could find employment and have a stake."</p>

