      credit="朝日新聞社 [Asahi Shimbun], 1951. Public domain."
      accent="pachinko"
    />

    <p>The stigma attached to pachinko — its association with gambling, quasi-legality, and Korean identity — was the product of the same discrimination that made Korean participation in the industry necessary. In the novel, this dynamic is shown through the cost of Mozasu's success and through Noa's tragic struggle to escape.</p>

    <h2>Japan's Economic Miracle and What It Did Not Change</h2>

    <p>The Japan of <em>Pachinko</em>'s later sections — the 1970s, 1980s, and the 1989 peak of the "bubble economy" — is one of the wealthiest countries in the world. The 1964 Tokyo Olympics announced Japan's postwar rebirth to a global audience. By the mid-1980s, Japan's GDP was the second largest on earth. Real estate and stock prices rose to levels that seemed impervious to gravity.</p>

    <p>The Zainichi community participated in this economy as workers and small business owners. They did not share proportionally in its prosperity. The discrimination that had structured their economic lives since the 1920s — in employment, housing, education, and legal status — did not dissolve when Japan became wealthy. In some respects it became more visible: the contrast between Japan's proclaimed modernity and the conditions of its largest ethnic minority grew sharper as the country grew richer.</p>

    <p>Solomon's storyline in 1989 straddles this contradiction. He is employed by a global bank, educated in America, and fluent in three languages — he is, by the logic of meritocracy, exactly what the system claims to reward. The land deal that ends his career in Japan fails not because of anything Solomon did wrong, but because of his inescapable legal and social identity: the system's logic has limits that apply to him and not to others. The economic miracle did not alleviate those limits. It simply made them harder to explain or justify.</p>

